// Practice quiz: Encapsulation with getters and setters
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/encapsulation-with-getters-and-setters

::o1 why private::Why make a field private and add a getter? {
~It makes the program run faster#Not quite. Access modifiers are about who may use a field, not speed.
~It lets subclasses change the field freely#Not quite. private hides the field from subclasses too.
=Other classes can read the value but cannot change it directly#Correct. The class keeps control of every change to its own state.
~It stops the value being read anywhere#Not quite. The getter is there exactly so others can read it.
}

::o1 getter shape::private int score; Which is the usual getter for this field? {
=public int getScore() \{ return score; \}#Correct. It is public, takes no parameters and returns the field.
~public void getScore() \{ return score; \}#Not quite. void cannot return a value, so this does not compile.
~private int getScore() \{ return score; \}#Not quite. A private getter cannot be used by other classes.
~public int getScore(int score) \{ return score; \}#Not quite. This just returns its own parameter, not the field.
}

::o2 reject value::private int age; Which setter body rejects a negative age and keeps the object valid? (isBad(age) is true when age is negative.) {
~this.age \= age; if (isBad(age)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("age");#Not quite. The bad value is already stored before the exception is thrown.
~if (isBad(age)) age \= 0; this.age \= age;#Not quite. This silently changes the caller value instead of reporting the mistake.
=if (isBad(age)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("age"); this.age \= age;#Correct. It checks first and stores the value only when it is valid.
~this.age \= Math.abs(age);#Not quite. Turning -5 into 5 hides the mistake and stores a value nobody asked for.
}

::o2 one place::Why put the validation inside the setter rather than in every class that calls it? {
~Callers are not allowed to use if statements#Not quite. They can; the point is that they might forget.
=One check guards every change, so no caller can put the object in a bad state#Correct. Callers cannot skip a check that lives inside the class.
~Setters run before the constructor#Not quite. The constructor runs first; setters run whenever they are called.
~It removes the need for a getter#Not quite. Reading and changing a value are separate jobs.
}

::o3 set once::An Account gets its accountNumber when it is created, and it must never change. How should the field be exposed? {
=Set it in the constructor, provide a getter and no setter#Correct. Without a setter nothing can change it after creation; making it final adds a compiler check.
~Provide a getter and a setter#Not quite. A setter would let any code change the number later.
~Make it public so it is easy to read#Not quite. A public field can also be changed by any class.
~Provide a setter that only works once#Not quite. That is more code and more risk than simply having no setter.
}

::o3 controlled change::balance should change only through deposit and withdraw, which check each amount. Should the class also have setBalance? {
~Yes; every private field needs a setter#Not quite. A setter is added only when free assignment is safe and wanted.
~Yes, as long as it is public#Not quite. Being public makes the problem worse, not better.
~No, because a private field cannot be changed at all#Not quite. Methods of the class itself can still change it.
=No; a setter would let code set any balance and skip those checks#Correct. deposit and withdraw are the only safe ways in.
}
